From 670e41b062675b78e41b8847a76ddb375d0c7c30 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: duncan0k <dev@ark0.si>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 22:45:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 81/88] consensus: deploy the P2MR rescue lane, closed on Ark-0
 until its height is decided

The rescue lane relaxes the rules, a hard fork: GetBlockScriptFlags sets
SCRIPT_VERIFY_P2MR_NO_RESCUE for every block below the height of the new
buried deployment p2mr_rescue, which ConnectBlock and the mempool's
second check both read.

- Ark-0: ARK0_RESCUE_HEIGHT, not decided yet, so it stays at
  std::numeric_limits<int>::max() and the lane closed, as M4-S did before
  its height was fixed; static assertions keep it above the tripwire and
  SLH-DSA heights. The height has to be announced at least four weeks
  ahead and never changes once a build with it has left the network's own
  nodes.
- Another custom signet opens it with -signetrescue=<height>, which needs
  -signetchallenge and -signettripwire, at or above the tripwire height,
  and is refused on Ark-0, on the default signet and on the other chains.
- Regtest opens it at the final p2mr_tripwire height, after both
  -testactivationheight and -signettripwire, so that the existing
  configurations that only move the tripwire height still start; an
  explicit -testactivationheight=p2mr_rescue@N below that height is
  refused once all options are read.
- The network id that proofs are bound to is SHA256 of the signet
  challenge, since every signet has the same genesis block, or
  SHA256("regtest"); none on mainnet and the testnets, where the lane
  never opens. ChainstateManager gives it to its ValidationCache, and
  CheckInputScripts to every transaction it checks.
- The startup log names the height and the network id, and
  getdeploymentinfo lists p2mr_rescue.
